# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in what you need.

# A free Google Books API key. Lookups technically work without one, but they go out anonymously
# against a small shared IP quota that returns 429 on days nobody has even opened the app — so in
# practice book search is unreliable until this is set. Open Library still answers as a fallback,
# so search degrades rather than breaks.
# Create one at https://console.cloud.google.com/ (enable the "Books API"), then restrict the key
# to the Books API — an *API restriction*, never an HTTP referrer one, which would reject every
# server-side call.
GOOGLE_BOOKS_API_KEY=

# Where the SQLite database file lives. Defaults to data/reading-tracker.db.
# Point this at a persistent volume when deploying (the Docker image sets it to /data/...).
# DATABASE_PATH=data/reading-tracker.db

# The public URL the app is served from, e.g. https://reading.example.com.
# REQUIRED when running behind Cloudflare Tunnel or any HTTPS reverse proxy — without it, SvelteKit
# rejects POST form actions (add book, mark finished, setup) as cross-origin. Not needed for local dev.
# ORIGIN=https://reading.example.com


# ---- Who may create an account ----
#
# The default is CLOSED: an instance with neither of the following set will refuse every sign-up
# after the first. That is deliberate — a server on the public internet with no configuration should
# refuse rather than accept, so the safe outcome is the one you get by doing nothing.
#
# The FIRST sign-up is always allowed regardless, and claims whatever household is already in the
# database. Without that, a fresh deploy would have nobody able to sign in and no way to fix it.

# Hand this to people you want to let in; they type it on the sign-up form. Leave unset to disable
# invite-based sign-up entirely.
# SIGNUP_INVITE_CODE=

# Let anyone with the URL create an account. Only sensible for an instance you intend to be a public
# service — on a home server this hands strangers a household on your box.
# ALLOW_OPEN_SIGNUP=false


# ---- Behind a proxy ----
#
# Login is rate limited per client IP as well as per email. Behind a reverse proxy or tunnel, every
# request appears to come from the proxy unless adapter-node is told which header carries the real
# address — without this the per-IP limit throttles all your users as if they were one.
# ADDRESS_HEADER=CF-Connecting-IP
